<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="peril-box"><p class="peril-h">Common local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Frozen pipes in vacant units</li><li>Kitchen &amp; electrical fires</li><li>Wind &amp; hail</li><li>Heavy snow</li><li>Water damage between units</li></ul></div>

<h2>A college-town claim profile</h2>
<p>State College borough and the surrounding townships, College, Ferguson, Patton and Harris, are shaped by Penn State. Older houses in the Highlands and Holmes-Foster neighborhoods have been converted to student rentals, apartment buildings line the streets near campus, and duplexes and townhouses fill the outlying areas. Further out, College Heights, Boalsburg and Bellefonte have owner-occupied homes ranging from stone and brick older houses to ranches and newer subdivisions.</p>
<p>Rental-heavy housing creates a particular set of claims. Units sit empty over winter break and the summer. Tenants turn the heat down, leave windows cracked or miss a dripping pipe. Kitchen fires, overloaded circuits and space heaters cause losses during the semester. And because one building often holds several units, water from one apartment frequently ends up in another.</p>
<h2>Frozen pipes over break</h2>
<p>Central Pennsylvania winters are cold enough that a heating failure in an empty unit can freeze pipes within a day or two. By the time tenants return, water may have run for days. Insurers may point to clauses about maintaining heat or about vacancy, and they may argue that damage developed over time instead of suddenly.</p>
<p>Those questions depend heavily on facts and policy wording, and some are coverage issues best taken to a Pennsylvania attorney. When coverage is accepted, disputes often shift to scope: how much drywall, flooring and cabinetry has to be replaced, whether drying was adequate, and what mold remediation is needed. See our <a href="/policy-and-claim-guides/ice-dam-and-frozen-pipe-claims/">ice dam and frozen pipe guide</a> for more.</p>
<h2>What landlords should track</h2>
<p>Landlord policies differ from homeowner policies in ways that matter after a loss. Common issues include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Loss of rents or fair rental value</strong>, especially when repairs push past the start of a lease.</li>
<li><strong>Tenant contents</strong>, which belong on the tenant's renters policy, not the building policy.</li>
<li><strong>Vacancy or unoccupancy clauses</strong> that may limit coverage for some perils.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades</strong> required by the borough or township when a unit is rebuilt.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rental turnover in a college town is tied to the academic calendar, so a delayed claim can cost a landlord an entire lease year. That's a real loss to document when the policy covers it.</p>
<h2>Storms in the ridge and valley</h2>
<p>Centre County sits among long Appalachian ridges, and storms can intensify as they cross them. On May 31, 1985, a powerful tornado tore through the Moshannon State Forest during a deadly outbreak across the region. More routinely, thunderstorms bring damaging winds and hail, and winter storms pile snow on flat apartment roofs. Our <a href="/property-appraisal-services/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">windstorm and tornado appraisal</a> and <a href="/property-appraisal-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">fire and smoke damage appraisal</a> services cover the losses we see most.</p>
<h2>How appraisal fits</h2>
<p>When you and the carrier agree there's a covered loss but disagree on the amount, the appraisal clause gives each side an appraiser, with an umpire to resolve anything they can't agree on. Pennsylvania courts generally treat appraisal awards much like common-law arbitration, which means they are hard to overturn absent fraud, misconduct or a similar irregularity. Remember that many policies limit lawsuits to one year from the date of loss.</p>

<h2>Services available in State College</h2>
<p>Every Pennsylvania Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in State College and the rest of Centre County, including:</p>
<ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/home-insurance-claim-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/commercial-hoa-claim-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/roof-hail-damage-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">Wind &amp; Tornado Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-remnant-appraisal/">Tropical Storm Remnant Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/water-damage-claim-appraisal/">Water Damage Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-appraisal-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from State College property owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Pipes froze in my rental over winter break. Is it covered?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often yes, but policies may require that heat be maintained or limit coverage for vacant units. The facts and wording matter. If coverage is accepted and the amount is disputed, appraisal can settle it.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my landlord policy cover my tenants&#x27; belongings?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually not. Tenants&#x27; personal property belongs on their own renters policy. Your landlord policy covers the building and any property you own and provide.</p></div></details></div>
